          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.068,
            0.488,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.113,
            0.401,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.159,
                0.329,
                0.368,
                0.346
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.28: A cyclic 4-gon"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.124,
            0.876,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": "The formula states that if the sides of the cyclic 4-gon have lengths $a ,   b ,   c ,   d ,$ and the semi-perimeter s is $\\frac{1}{2}(a + b + c + d)$ , then: Area\u001bof $4-\\mathrm{gen}=\\sqrt{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.261,
            0.877,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": "It is an amazing and beautiful formula! Does it not remind us of Heron’s formula for the area of a triangle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.355,
            0.883,
            0.394
          ],
          "content": "We can verify that the formula works out correctly in various special cases."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.394,
            0.884,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": "**Example 6:** Verify Brahmagupta’s formula for the case of a rectangle. All rectangles are cyclic, so Brahmagupta’s formula should apply. Let the sides of the rectangle be a, b. Then $s = \\frac{1}{2}(2a + 2b) = a + b$ , so the formula yields:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.503,
            0.825,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Area}=\\sqrt{(a+b-a)(a+b-b)(a+b-a)(a+b-b)}=\\sqrt{ab\\cdot ab}=ab,",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.549,
            0.884,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "which is correct. Please check out the formula against other special cases. **Example 7:** Verify Brahmagupta’s formula for the case of an isosceles trapezium."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.626,
            0.461,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.171,
                0.763,
                0.429,
                0.781
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.29: Isosceles trapezium"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_20_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.482,
            0.621,
            0.875,
            0.72
          ],
          "content": "All isosceles trapezia are cyclic, hence the formula should apply. The perimeter is $2 a \\; + \\; 2 b \\; + \\; \\bar { 2 c } _ { \\mathrm { s } }$ , so $s = a + b + c.$ . Hence, the area as given by the formula should be"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.528,
            0.725,
            0.829,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": "\\sqrt{(s - 2a)(s - 2b)(s - c)(s - c)}",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.483,
            0.763,
            0.875,
            0.822
          ],
          "content": "Since $s - 2a = c + b - a, \\; s - 2b = c + a$ $-2b,   s-c=a+b$ the above formula simplifies to"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.241,
            0.829,
            0.754,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": "(a+b)\\sqrt{(c+b-a)(c+a-b)}=(a+b)\\sqrt{c^2-(b-a)^2}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_20_equation_15.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
